<Guide lang="en" title="Islam, for those close to Buddhism" description="A guide to Islam for Buddhists and people at home in Buddhist culture: taming the mind, precepts, prayer and fasting as practice.">
  <p>If you go to a temple, or have found peace of mind on a templestay, Islam may feel very far away. Buddhism does not speak of a creator God, while Islam believes in one God. Yet in their attitude to life and their practice, the two traditions are closer than you might think.</p>

  <h2>Where they meet</h2>
  <h3>Letting go of greed and attachment</h3>
  <p>Buddhism sees greed, anger and ignorance (the three poisons) as the roots of suffering. In Islam, many scholars call the struggle to master one's own ego and desires (nafs) the greatest "jihad", the greatest effort. Not being bound by wealth and status, and living humbly, is a path both traditions point to.</p>

  <h3>Precepts and ethics</h3>
