  <h3>Practice with the body</h3>
  <p>Muslim prayer, five times a day, includes bowing down with the forehead on the ground, much like the prostrations made at a temple. The month of fasting in Ramadan is a practice of emptying the mind through hunger, and washing before prayer is a way of settling the heart. <a href="/en/questions/prayer/">Muslim prayer</a> · <a href="/en/questions/ramadan/">Ramadan</a></p>

  <h3>Compassion</h3>
  <p>Like compassion in Buddhism, the most important name of God in Islam is "the Most Merciful" (ar-Rahman). Almost every chapter of the Qur'an opens with "In the name of God, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."</p>

  <h2>Where they differ</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A creator.</strong> Buddhism does not speak of a god who creates and governs the universe; Islam believes in one God who created everything.</li>
    <li><strong>Awakening and revelation.</strong> In Buddhism, one reaches awakening through one's own practice. In Islam, God shows the way through prophets, and people follow it while relying on God's mercy.</li>
    <li><strong>Rebirth and resurrection.</strong> Buddhism teaches cycles of birth and death until nirvana; Islam teaches one life followed by resurrection and judgement.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These differences run deep. Even so, Muslims feel there is much to learn from Buddhists' restraint, calm and care for all living things.</p>

  <h2>Parents and ancestors</h2>
  <p>Filial piety, cherished in Korean Buddhist families, is a great value in Islam too. Right after the command to worship God, the Qur'an says "be good to your parents" (17:23). The Prophet Muhammad taught that "paradise lies at the feet of mothers." The difference is that Muslims do not bow to or hold memorial rites (jesa) for the dead; instead they pray to God for them and do good deeds in their name.</p>

